# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A workflow sequences commands and queries. It spans several transactions, and opens
# none of its own** — each command it calls opens exactly one.
#
# That is a saga, not a unit of work, and the bill comes with it:
#
# - **Every step is idempotent**, because the only recovery from a half-run sequence is to
#   run it again.
# - **Every intermediate state is a legal state.** Stopping at step three of six must leave
#   the world consistent, just less far along.
# - **Undo is another step, not a rollback.** A compensating command is written into the
#   sequence, named and tested like any other, and it is forward motion: you cannot
#   un-send a confirmation, you send a cancellation.
#
# It answers with a Result, and the class method enforces that.
#
# **A workflow does not branch.** Branching is deciding, and a rule has one home. The three
# legal ways to express a condition: stop on `failure`, push the condition into a command
# that no-ops convergently, or choose which workflow to run at the edge.
#
# **A workflow does not call a workflow.** Its whole content is its sequence, and nesting
# hides the only thing it had to offer.
#
# **Every permission is checked before the first step runs.** A workflow spans several
# transactions, so a refusal partway cannot undo what came before — those transactions
# closed. Step three refusing leaves steps one and two done, committed and visible, with no
# rollback. Before the first step is the only moment refusing is free.
#
# **The steps are read out of `call`, not declared.** They were a `STEPS` constant for a
# while, and a constant beside the method that already lists them is the same fact written
# twice — guarded by a cop, but still two places to change and one of them easy to forget.
# `RubyVM::AbstractSyntaxTree.of` hands back the syntax tree of `call` itself, so the list is
# derived from the only copy there is and cannot disagree with it.
#
# **Only `call` is read, and that is a constraint worth having.** A workflow *is* a sequence;
# a step hidden behind a private helper is a sequence that does not read as one.
#
# **Anything the walker cannot see is a step whose permission is never demanded**, so the
# reading and the cop have to recognise the same shapes or the build is green over a
# fail-open. `Shipshape/WorkflowAggregatesPermissions` fails a step this cannot read — a
# receiver that is not a constant, or an operation called from a method other than `call` —
# rather than describing it as a limit and leaving it to production.
class Workflow
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission

  # Ask a workflow what it needs without running it: hide a button the actor cannot use,
  # populate an admin screen, seed the permission table. Read out of `call`, so it cannot
  # disagree with what the workflow actually does — there is only one copy.
  #
  # **Anonymous steps contribute nothing.** An anonymous operation is never granted — that
  # is what anonymous means — so aggregating its name would demand a grant nobody can hold
  # and make the whole workflow permanently forbidden.
  # **A workflow contributes no permission of its own**, which is the one place it differs
  # from every other operation: granting `:SettleMonth` does nothing, because a workflow is
  # only the acts it sequences. Its steps aggregate the same way anything else does.
  def self.permissions(seen = [])
    return [] if anonymous? || seen.include?(self)

    onward = seen + [self]
    found = steps
    # **A workflow with no steps is not a workflow**, and answering `[]` here would be the
    # fail-open this model must never allow: `[].all?` is true, so it would run for an actor
    # holding no grants at all. That is also what a step hidden behind a private helper looks
    # like from here, which is why the shape is refused rather than tolerated.
    # An anonymous workflow is unchecked by declaration, so an empty list is its intent rather
    # than the fail-open this raise exists to refuse.
    raise NotImplementedError, "#{name}#call names no operations to run" if found.empty? && !anonymous?

    found.flat_map { |step| step.permissions(onward) }.uniq
  end

  # The operations `call` names, read by `Calls` — the same reader the call graph uses, so a
  # step and an edge are one fact found one way. Memoised per class: the tree is parsed from the
  # file on disk, and a workflow is asked this on every invocation.
  def self.steps
    return @steps if instance_variable_defined?(:@steps)

    @steps = Calls.in(self, anonymous? ? :anonymous_call : :call)
  end

  # `permits?` is inherited and needs no override: every operation demands what it reaches,
  # and a workflow's steps are that same aggregation. **One predicate, asked twice** — a view
  # asks it to decide whether to offer the button, `call` asks it to refuse, and there is no
  # second answer to get out of step with the first.

<%- end -%>
<%- if auth -%>
  # A workflow every one of whose steps is anonymous is itself anonymous — a signup
  # sequence runs before anyone is identified — and says so the same way an operation does,
  # by implementing `anonymous_call`.
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless permits?(actor)
      # **The refusal is the entry nobody has when they need it.** Recorded before returning,
      # because a caller who was told no is exactly what somebody comes looking for.
      AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: :refused, actor: actor, error: :forbidden,
                      arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)
      return Result.failure(:forbidden)
    end

    operation = new(**arguments)
    result = operation.__perform__(actor)
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    result = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

<%- if auth -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the steps, before answering.** A workflow opens no transaction of its own — the
    # steps did — so what this must not sit inside is a step, where a rollback there would
    # erase the entry describing the sequence.
    AuditLog.record(
      operation: name,
      outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
      actor: actor,
      error: result.error,
      arguments: arguments,
      excluded: excluded_from_audit,
    )

    result
  end
<%- else -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the steps, before answering.** A workflow opens no transaction of its own — the
    # steps did — so what this must not sit inside is a step, where a rollback there would
    # erase the entry describing the sequence.
    AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
                    error: result.error, arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)

    result
  end
<%- end -%>

  private
<%- if auth -%>

  # Whoever the door was opened for. Most operations never read it, which is why it is here
  # rather than in every initializer.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  def success(value = nil)
    Result.success(value)
  end

  def failure(error)
    Result.failure(error)
  end

  # **Dispatches with an implicit receiver**, so it works whether the operation left its
  # entry point public or made it private. `send` would work too and is banned: it steps
  # over `private`, routes around the call graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A
  # base class that writes it cannot ask an application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on — and `Shipshape/OnlyTheDoorIsCalled` refuses the attempt at
  # the call site, where no visibility keyword can be stepped over.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** The check
    # above needed it; most operations do not, and forcing `actor:` into every initializer
    # would put a keyword in fifty constructors so that three could read it. Set here, and
    # available to any operation that wants it through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
  end
<%- else -%>
  public def __perform__
    call
  end
<%- end -%>

  # **The only way in is `call`.** A caller cannot build an operation, so it cannot reach one
  # that skipped the permission check, the transaction or the return-type assertion.
  #
  # `allocate` is on the list because it is the hole `new` alone leaves: it is public on every
  # Ruby class, it skips `initialize` entirely, and `SettleInvoice.allocate.__perform__(actor)`
  # ran the operation unauthenticated. Verified by running it, not by reading it.
  # **What this operation keeps out of the trail**, beyond what `PersonalData` already
  # declares. For a value that is not a column and so is in no registry: a token, a free-text
  # note, a payload.
  def self.excluded_from_audit
    const_defined?(:EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT, false) ? const_get(:EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT, false) : []
  end

  private_class_method :excluded_from_audit

  private_class_method :new, :allocate
end
